ction \"DOWN\"? Hmm, but many tutorials say for bottom sheet use \"Move In\" with direction \"Down\". Yes — I'm fairly confident Figma prototyping \"Move In Down\" slides the frame up from the bottom edge (the arrow ↓ points at the direction of origin... whatever, tutorials confirm Down for bottom sheets). Hmm, actually now I doubt. Let me recall the Figma education material: \"To create a modal that slides up from the bottom, use Move In and select the up arrow\"? \n\nI genuinely can't be 100% sure. It's a prototype nicety; the evaluation focuses on links and completeness. I'll use `navigation: \"OVER\"` with `transition: { type: \"MOVE_IN\", direction: \"DOWN\" }` for the sheet (matching common bottom-sheet tutorials), and `navigation: \"CHANGE_TO\"`... no, keep NAVIGATE for others. For the menu (slide from left): \"OVER\" + MOVE_IN direction \"RIGHT\"? Menu slides in from left → enters from left side → direction \"LEFT\" (if direction = side of entry) or \"RIGHT\" (if direction = movement direction, moving rightward). Tutorials for side drawer from left: \"Move In ←\"? Hmm.\n\nLet me just decide: direction indicates the direction of motion of the incoming frame. Bottom sheet: moves UP → \"UP\". Left drawer: moves RIGHT → \"RIGHT\". Hmm, but Figma UI shows direction arrows that visually match \"from where it comes\"...\n\nActually — I can resolve this with certainty by checking Figma's own transition presets? Not easily. Alternative: keep it simple and safe: use NAVIGATE with SMART_ANIMATE/DISSOLVE for everything, and for the sheet use OVER + MOVE_IN \"DOWN\". You know what — I recall clearly now from Figma's docs page \"Explore prototyping features\": for a bottom sheet they demonstrate \"Move in / down\"? I'll go with DOWN for sheet and LEFT for menu-drawer-from-left... \n\nHmm wait. Let me think about \"Move Out\" semantics: Move Out = current frame moves out revealing destination. If you do Move In with direction DOWN, does the incoming frame slide down from the top or up from the bottom? \n\nI found it — I remember the Figma best-practice: iOS modal sheet in Figma = \"Move In\" + direction \"Up\"? No wait...\n\nOK let me think of it differently. In Figma's UI, the direction picker for Move In shows arrows: ← means the new frame enters from the right moving left? or from the left moving right? Test memory: People making an onboarding carousel \"push left\" use Push with → (new frame comes from the right pushing old to left; movement is leftward; the arrow points left = direction of movement). So arrow = direction of motion. Bottom sheet moves up → arrow UP → \"UP\". Left drawer moves right → \"RIGHT\". \n\nHmm, but for Push: \"Push ←\" moves frames leftward — arrow = motion direction. OK: sheet = MOVE_IN + UP, menu drawer from left = MOVE_IN + RIGHT. I'll go with that. If it looks wrong in presentation, it's still a working prototype link; evaluation is on links/completeness.\n\nAlso \"Presentation: sheet over 01\" — I could set the 02 frame's own background to show 01 behind it (static), which I'm doing anyway. The prototype link from 01's Get Started goes to 02 with OVER/MOVE_IN UP which visually slides the sheet-like frame up. Good enough. Actually — with NAVIGATE vs OVER: OVER keeps the previous frame underneath in presentation (real overlay). Since 02 already has baked-in background, either works. OVER + MOVE_IN UP gives the authentic sheet slide. I'll use OVER for 01→02 and 03→04 (menu), NAVIGATE for 02→03 (close sheet → home; actually Swap? NAVIGATE fine) and 02→01 (Cancel, NAVIGATE with MOVE_OUT DOWN? keep DISSOLVE simple... I'll use NAVIGATE + MOVE_OUT with direction DOWN for cancel = sheet dismisses downward). Keep it reasonable.\n\nNow — component instances of media: they're COMPONENT nodes (4:5 etc.), so I can `component.createInstance()`.\n\nText Field focused state: brief requires the phone field focused. I'll build a custom focused input: white fill, 2px brand border, +91 prefix + divider + placeholder. I can instance the Text Field component and override, but the +91 prefix needs an added child. Instances allow adding children? No — instances can't have children added unless the main component has slots... Actually you CAN'T add children to an instance in the Plugin API (only if using componentProperty-driven?). Hmm: Instances don't support appendChild. Right — \"You cannot add or remove children of an instance.\" So for the focused phone field with +91, I'll build it as my own component (\"Text Field / Phone · Focused\") based on the same visual language (white input, radius 12, brand border). The brief says default Text Field given; other states are mine to design as reusable components. \n\nSo in my workspace I'll create local components:\n- `Button / Primary` — instance of given Button? The given Button IS a component; I can use instances of it directly, and create variants via overrides (text swap). Button text override is fine (\"Cancel\"? No — Cancel is a text button on sheet header). For onboarding Get Started + phone Get Started: instance of Button 1:23, override label to \"Get Started\" (already is). \n- `Text Field / Phone · Focused` — custom component with label, focused input with +91.\n- `App Shell / Status Bar` — use instance of 1:33; recolor glyphs white where on dark areas. Instances allow overriding fills of children (via instance.findOne and setting fills — allowed as override). For 03_Home with a dark brand header area... My design: status bar on light bg → black glyphs fine. Balance card is brand-colored but status bar sits above it on light bg. OK, no recolor needed. 01: light bg, black glyphs. 02: sheet is light; the dimmed background at top — status bar area over dimmed onboarding: keep black glyphs (dim scrim over light bg still light enough? scrim 40% black over light bg → medium gray; black glyphs still visible). Fine.\n- Business components (reusable): \n  - `Action Tile` (icon + label)\n  - `Reward Banner`\n  - `Account Tile` (mark + name + balance)\n  - `Transaction Row` (leading mark, title, subtitle, amount)\n  - `Menu Item` (icon + label + chevron?)\n  - `Home Header` (avatar + search + history)\n  - `Balance Card`\n  - `Side Menu`\n  \nBuilding everything as components + instances adds complexity; but \"Component reuse\" is an evaluation criterion. I'll create a reasonable set of local components in the workspace (components section off-canvas) and use instances on frames. Transaction Row needs per-row overrides (text + image swaps) — instances with text overrides are fine; the leading mark differs (Paytm mark is 72×72 image?, avatars, amazon 26×26). Let me check what the merchant marks look like — Merchant_Paytm symbol is 72×72, Merchant_Amazon 26×26. Inside transaction row I'd have a 44×44 chip; I can swap instance children... In an instance, swapping a nested component instance is possible: `instance.findOne(...).swapComponent(otherComponent)`? The Plugin API has `instanceNode.swapComponent(componentNode)` on InstanceNode? There's `swapComponent` on `InstanceNode`? Hmm — `InstanceNode.swapComponent(componentNode: ComponentNode)` exists? I recall `instance.swapComponent()` was added (2023?). If unsure, alternative: design Transaction Row with a \"slot\" concept — build each row as an instance but override the leading icon by swapping. If swapComponent isn't available, I could instead build rows directly (not instances) — but then less component reuse. Middle ground: make Transaction Row a component; on Home, use 3 instances; swap the leading icon child per instance. Let me test swapComponent in a probe script; if unavailable, fallback: set instance child visible false and... can't add. Alternative approach: make the leading \"media chip\" part of the row component, and for each row instance, swap using `swapComponent`. I'll probe.\n\nActually, simpler robust approach: Transaction Row component has a 40×40 rounded chip frame with a nested placeholder instance. For each row instance: find the placeholder instance inside, call swapComponent(targetComponent), then resize to fit (Paytm 72×72 → scale to 28? marks keep brand colors; sizes: Paytm 72, Amazon 26, avatar Risa 48×72). Hmm — resizing an instance inside an instance via override is allowed (resize children of instance = override). OK.\n\nLet me probe: check `typeof instance.swapComponent === 'function'`.\n\nAlso, I should check media component internals: Bank_HSBC is 75×21 (wide logo), others 26×26. Merchant_Paytm 72×72. In Account Tile (mark + name + balance), tile ~163×62: mark 24-26, name 13px, balance 14 Semi Bold. HSBC mark is wide 75×21 — scale down to fit 28×28 chip? Scaling non-uniformly distorts. HSBC: resize to 32×9? Keep aspect: 34×9.5. In a 28 chip that's fine visually (white chip w/ red logo?). HSBC logo has red/white; keep its colors per brief.\n\nLet me look at what the marks actually are (vector logos with fills). I'll trust them.\n\nAlso need Avatar_Risa_Midyett (48×72) — taller than wide? Odd. 48×72. In a 40 chip, resize to 40×40 might distort (aspect 2:3). Hmm. Avatar images are likely ellipse-cropped photos? Let me inspect 4:2 and 4:3 and the merchants to understand. Let me also inspect Illustration (4:5).\n\nI'll write one more read-only probe for: 4:2, 4:3, 4:4, 4:24, 4:11, 4:5 internals (shallow), plus test whether reactions are writable on a scratch node? Reactions test: create temp rect in workspace? That writes — but only temporarily; I can remove it. That's acceptable (\"read-only use_figma to inspect\" — the instructions say inspect via read-only, build via use_figma; a tiny throwaway test is fine but let me avoid; I'll just attempt reactions assignment during build and handle errors).\n\nAlso check: `figma.getLocalComponents`? Not needed.\n\nFont loading: need `await figma.loadFontAsync({family:\"Inter\", style:\"Regular\"})`, \"Medium\", \"Semi Bold\", \"Bold\". Inter styles: Regular, Medium, Semi Bold, Bold.\n\nNote: Button text uses fontName Inter Semi Bold 16 — to override instance text I must load that font first.\n\nMedia in workspace: To use media components in my workspace frames, I can directly create instances of the reference components (they live in the same file). The brief says \"Copy these components into your own file\" — since we're in the same file (workspace page), I can either clone the components into workspace or just instance the originals. Instancing originals is cleanest and preserves reference pages (no modification). But \"build them as reusable components in your own file\" refers to business components — I'll create those in the Workspace page. For media, instances of the original components are fine (and required: \"use them as instances\").\n\nHmm — one consideration: cloning media components into the workspace page could duplicate; unnecessary. Instances of 4:x components from reference page = normal Figma usage. Good.\n\n**Plan of build steps:**\n\nStep 1: Setup workspace page + local components (colors as paint styles? Optional — paint styles would be nice: create local paint styles for the palette. Quick win for \"consistency\". Maybe skip to save calls; manual consistency is fine. Actually paint styles are cheap to create and help evaluation \"consistency of your own system\". I'll create ~12 paint styles + use them.)\n\nHmm, keep scope manageable. Styles: I'll create them (one call), apply key ones.\n\nStep 2: Local components:\n- Action Tile (Send/Receive/Scan)\n- Account Tile\n- Transaction Row\n- Reward Banner\n- Menu Item\n- Phone Field Focused (with +91)\n- Section Header? (simple text, skip component)\n- Side Menu drawer (component containing menu items) — could build directly on frame; but reusable component is good. I'll build drawer as component `Side Menu`.\n\nStep 3: Frames:\n- 01_Onboarding\n- 02_Phone_Number (01 clone + scrim + sheet + keypad)\n- 03_Home\n- 04_Menu (03 clone + scrim + drawer)\n\nStep 4: Prototype reactions.\n\nStep 5: Screenshots + fixes.\n\nLayout coordinates on workspace page: place frames in a row: 01 at x=0, 02 at x=470, 03 at x=940, 04 at x=1410, y=0. Components section below at y=1000.\n\nClone for backgrounds: For 02, clone the finished 01 frame, set position inside 02 at (0,0), name it \"01_Onboarding (dimmed)\" and lock? Frames can't be children? Frames CAN be nested in frames. A frame child of frame is fine. Set its opacity/dim via a scrim rect on top. Cloning a frame with instances inside = fine (instances clone as instances).\n\nBut careful: clone the 01 frame AFTER it's fully built. Same for 03 → 04. Build order: 01, 03 first; then 02 (clones 01) and 04 (clones 03).\n\n**03_Home detailed layout** (390×844), light theme:\n- bg #F5F6FA\n- Status bar instance (0,0), black glyphs\n- Header (y=51, x=16): avatar instance 44×44 (resize from 72 — instance resize OK) at (16,56); search field: x=68..332 w=? avatar 44 + gap 10 → search from 70 to 390-16-40-10: history icon button 40 at right (334..374). Search w=244? Let me: avatar (16,56,44,44), search (70,56,238,44 radius 22, bg white, border #E7E8EE): Icon_Search 18px at left inset 14, text \"Pay by name or phone\" 14 Regular #9A9AA3 at x=102? icon at 84.. wait search x=70, icon inside at x=84 (14 inset) y=69 (18px), text at x=108, centered vertically y=56+13=... text y=70 h=17? 44 high field: text 14px line ~17 → y=69.5. OK.\n- history button: circle 44 at (316? ) wait: 390-16-44=330. search from 70 to 330-8=322 → w=252. history circle at (330,56,44,44) white bg, Icon_History 20 centered.\n- Balance card (16,116, 358×150) brand #1E32FA radius 20, white text:\n  - HSBC mark: white chip rounded 8 at (20,20,36,36)? HSBC mark aspect 75:21 ≈ 3.57. In chip 36×36, resize mark to 28×8? too thin. Alternative: put mark at left as-is resized to w=40 h=11 at (20,24). Hmm. Better: top row: Bank_HSBC mark (w=42,h=12) at (20,22), then \"Primary\" pill right-aligned: pill h=24 at (..,20): Icon_Check_Badge 14 white + \"Primary\" 12 Medium white, pill bg white 20% opacity radius 12.\n  - \"Last Updated Balance\" 12 Medium white 70% at (20,72)\n  - ₹85,625: 32 Bold white at (20,92) h=39\n  - refresh: 40×40 circle white 16% at (314-... right: x=358-20-40=298? card x=16 w=358 → right inner edge 374-20=354... circle at (298, 96, 40,40)? Align with amount row: y=92 h=40. Icon_Refresh 20 white centered.\n- Actions row y=282: three tiles: Send, Receive, Scan any QR. Widths: Send/Receive w=159 each, scan w=? Let me do: Send (16,282,158,64), Receive (182,282,158,64)? then scan below? Hmm \"Scan any QR\" with Icon_Scan. Alternative composition: Send & Receive as two big pill buttons (brand for Send, green for Receive) + Scan as a square dark button on the right: row: Send (16,282,120,64), Receive (144,282,120,64), Scan (272,282,86,64)? Widths: 16+120+8+120+8+86=358 ✓ (16..374).\n  - Send tile: brand bg, white icon+label stacked? 64 high: icon 22 + label 13. Or horizontal icon+label. Stacked: icon at center-top (x=60-11), y=10, label y=36. 120 wide: \"Send\" fine. Scan tile w=86: label \"Scan any QR\" 12px two words... \"Scan any QR\" at 12 Medium might overflow 86 (≈70px width needed — ok).\n  Actually cleaner: 3 equal tiles (16,282,114,64) gap 8: 114*3+16=358 ✓. \"Scan any QR\" in 114 wide tile at 12px = fine.\n  - Send: brand #1E32FA bg; Receive: green #0BA678; Scan: ink #1A1A1F bg (dark) — scan stands out as utility. \n- Reward banner (16,354,358,56): bg #EEF1FF (brand tint) radius 16, Icon_Offer 24 brand at (16+16, 16), text \"Invite friends and get ₹1,000 reward\" 14 Semi Bold ink at x=68, chevron? skip.\n- Accounts section: header \"YOUR ACCOUNTS\" 11 Semi Bold letterspaced #6B6B76 at (16,430); grid tiles y=452: tile w=175, h=62, 2 cols gap 8: (16,452), (199,452), (16,522), (199,522). Tile: white radius 14, mark 26×26 at (14,12)... tile h=62: mark chip 32×32 at (12,15)? Let me: mark at (12, y+15) 32×32 rounded 8 bg #F5F6FA; name 13 Semi Bold ink at x=52 y=12; balance 14 Semi Bold ink? balance 13 Medium #1A1A1F at x=52 y=32. Wait h=62: name y=12 (h16), balance y=33 (h16). OK.\n  - Tiles: Axis Bank ₹3,500; HDFC Bank ₹63,520; Deutsche Bank ₹36,258; Union Bank ₹67,820.\n- Transactions: header row: \"RECENT TRANSACTIONS\" 11 Semi Bold #6B6B76 letterspaced at (16,600); \"Transaction History\" 13 Semi Bold brand right-aligned (374-…) — this is the required action \"Transaction History\". \n- Rows y=624: 3 rows h=56 gap 4: white radius 14 w=358. Row content: mark chip 36×36 at (12,10); title 14 Semi Bold; subtitle 12 Regular #6B6B76; amount right: \"−₹3,500\" 14 Semi Bold red-ish #DE3730? negative red, positive green #0BA678. Rows:\n  1. Paytm Auto Pay · 22 Jan · Weekly transaction · −₹3,500 (Merchant_Paytm)\n  2. Risa Midyett · 05 Jan · Gift for Christmas · +₹6,950 (Avatar_Risa_Midyett)\n  3. Amazon Pantry · 01 Jan · Subscription payment · −₹1,200 (Merchant_Amazon)\n  Title at x=60 y=9; subtitle y=29. Amount right-aligned x to 374-14=360: text width ~70 at (286,18)?\n  Rows at y=624, 684, 744 → ends 800. Home indicator zone 810-844. \n\nTotal: fits. \n\nWait — accounts \"each with its mark and balance\" and separately Balance: Bank_HSBC with \"Last Updated Balance\" ₹85,625 \"Primary\". So the 4 tiles are the other accounts. Good. Maybe label section \"YOUR OTHER ACCOUNTS\"? Brief: \"Accounts: Axis Bank · HDFC Bank · Deutsche Bank · Union Bank, each with its mark and balance (see DATA)\". Section header text is mine. I'll use \"OTHER ACCOUNTS\" hmm or \"ALL BANK ACCOUNTS\". Menu has \"Bank Accounts\". I'll use \"YOUR OTHER ACCOUNTS\". Keep simple: \"OTHER ACCOUNTS\".\n\n**01_Onboarding layout** (390×844) light:\n- bg #F5F6FA? Or white with brand blob. Let me: bg white; big brand-colored display words. Illustration 178×260 centered scaled to ~240 wide? Keep aspect 178:260 → 240×351 too big. Scale 200×292 at x=95 y=96. \n- Status bar (0,0).\n- Illustration centered (95,100,200,292).\n- Headline \"Pay\" \"Manage\" \"Grow…\" each line: display 44 Bold? Use 40 Bold, ink; place left-aligned x=24 y=436; line height 48: Pay y=436, Manage y=484, Grow… y=532 in brand color? Make \"Grow…\" brand. Hmm brief: 'Copy: \"Pay / Manage / Grow…\" (one word per line)'. So three text lines. \n- Subtitle \"An easy app to manage all your payment and finance-related needs\" 15 Regular #6B6B76 at (24,600) w=310 (2 lines).\n- Button instance (16,664,358,52) \"Get Started\".\n- \"Already have an account?\" row centered y=740: text 14 Medium #6B6B76 + \"Sign in\" 14 Semi Bold brand. Two text nodes or one with mixed? One text \"Already have an account?  Sign in\" with range colors — setRangeFills. Simpler: two text nodes side by side centered: compute widths? \"Already have an account?\" at 14 ≈ 170px, \"Sign in\" ≈ 47. Total ~225 → start x=(390-225)/2≈82. I'll set autoResize and position programmatically: set both, then measure and adjust. Easier: single text node with two segments and setRangeFills (brand for \"Sign in\"). Range styling on a text node: node.setRangeFills(24, 31, brand). Length: \"Already have an account?  Sign in\" — index of \"Sign in\" = 25 (if two spaces) to 32. I'll compute from characters.indexOf(\"Sign in\").\n- Home indicator (0,810).\n- Get Started → reaction to 02.\n\n**02_Phone_Number layout**:\n- bg: clone of 01 (full 844) at (0,0) + scrim black 45% over all.\n- Sheet (0,180,390,664) white, top radius 28, subtle shadow.\n  - grabber (177,10,36,5) #E1E3EA.\n  - Cancel text button top-right (.., 18): \"Cancel\" 15 Medium brand at right x≈318..374? width ~56 → x=302, y=18.\n  - Title \"Get Started\" 22 Bold ink at (24,52).\n  - Subtitle \"Please enter the mobile phone number\" 14 Regular #6B6B76 at (24,84).\n  - Phone field (focused): my component instance at (16,128) w=358: label \"Phone Number\" 14 Semi Bold ink; input box (16,153,358,52) white, border 2 brand, radius 12: \"+91\" 16 Semi Bold ink at x=16; divider 1×24 #E7E8EE at x=58; placeholder \"9812345678\" 16 Regular #9A9AA3 at x=70; caret? A 2×22 brand caret before placeholder? Focused empty → caret at 70. Add caret 2×22 brand at (70,15)? then placeholder shifts to x=78. Nice touch.\n  - Get Started button instance (16,236,358,52).\n  - Keypad instance (0, 844-226=618, 390,226) — inside sheet coordinates: sheet y=180 → keypad at sheet-local y=438. Keypad is full-width 390 ✓.\n  - Sheet contains: grabber, cancel, title, subtitle, field, button, keypad. Between button (ends 288) and keypad (starts 438 local) there's a gap 150 — fine (white sheet area).\n  Hmm — better: move field down: title 52, subtitle 86, field label 140, input 165..217, button 241..293. Gap to keypad 438: 145px empty. Could center content more: shift things down 40: title 80, subtitle 114, label 170, input 195..247, button 271..323, gap 115. OK do that. Or reduce sheet height: sheet from y=204 (h=640). Scrim shows more onboarding. Let me sheet y=204: local coords: grabber 10, cancel 20, title 64, subtitle 98, label 150, input 175..227, button 251..303, keypad 640-226=414..640. Gap 111. Fine.\n- Reactions: Get Started → 03 (NAVIGATE), Cancel → 01 (NAVIGATE).\n\n**04_Menu layout**:\n- bg: clone of 03 + scrim black 45%.\n- Drawer (0,0,312,844) white, right edge shadow. \n  - Status bar inside drawer? The drawer covers left 312 of screen; status bar of home is behind scrim. Menu content: top padding 64 (below notch): Avatar 64×64 at (24,72); \"Hallie Goebel\" 18 Semi Bold ink at (24,148); email 13 Regular #6B6B76 at (24,172).\n  - Divider (24,204,264,1) #E7E8EE.\n  - Menu items y start 220, h=52 each, gap 4: items with icon 22 + label 15 Medium ink + (chevron? no chevron icon available; skip or use \">\" text? skip).\n    - New Payment (Icon_New_Payment) y=220\n    - Bank Accounts (Icon_Bank) y=276\n    - All Transactions (Icon_History) y=332\n    - Rewards & Offers (Icon_Offer) y=388\n    - Settings (Icon_Settings) y=444\n  - Logout at bottom: divider + row at y=740: Icon_Logout 22 + \"Logout\" 15 Semi Bold red #DE3730.\n  - Icon color: #1A1A1F default; recolor icons in drawer to ink. Menu item icons maybe brand? Keep ink