rely IF swap preserves layout. Icon components are all 24×24 native; the instance in the component was resized to 22×22. If swap resets to 24×24 centered? It would keep x,y (46,12) but size 24 → offset slightly (46+24=70 > 114-46=68; slightly off-center by 2px). Acceptable-ish, but better: make the swap clean by deleting the nested icon instance from the tile instance?? Can't remove children from instances.\n\nAlternative robust approach: avoid swapping nested icons inside instances. Instead, give the Action Tile component a variant structure: build THREE separate tile instances directly on the frame as custom frames? But then \"component reuse\" — the Action Tile component exists and is used once (Send). Hmm.\n\nBetter approach: Instead of nested-instance swapping, hide the icon inside the instance? Can't add/remove children of instance. But CAN toggle visibility of nested children? Visibility override on nested children IS allowed via API? Setting `visible=false` on an instance's child — allowed as override ✓ (standard). But adding a NEW icon on top of the tile instance (as sibling on the frame) — yes! Overlay approach: keep tile instance (bg overridden, label overridden ✓ — text overrides worked... wait did they? The error happened before lb.characters? Order in my code: tile2.fills → ic.swapComponent → ic.resize → ic.x (ERROR). So text override untested but should work — text overrides inside instances are standard ✓).\n\nPlan B for icons inside instances: don't touch the nested icon at all; instead place the correct icon instance as a sibling ON THE FRAME above the tile, and set the nested icon's visible=false. Positioning nested-child visibility: allowed. Sibling icon on frame: full control ✓.\n\nBut wait — is `visible` override allowed on nested instance children? Yes, standard: \"You can override instance children properties: fills, visible, text, etc.\" ✓.\n\nHmm, but there's a subtlety: does the fills override on tile2 (root of instance) work? tile2.fills=[green] — instance root fill override ✓ standard.\n\nSimilarly for Account Tiles: the bank mark nested instance swap + reposition failed potentially (mk.resize(24,24) — resize might also fail inside instance? The error hit at tile2 first, so account tiles never executed. To be safe, use the same overlay strategy: hide nested mark, place a real mark instance on the frame over the chip? Positioning on frame at absolute coords — computable: tile at (x,y); chip at tile-local (12,15) → frame coords (x+12, y+15); mark 24×24 centered in 32×32 chip → (x+16, y+19). Doable.\n\nFor transaction rows: same — hide nested merchant, overlay avatar/amazon on frame at chip coords: row at (16,y): chip (16+12, y+10) size 36; avatar 24×36 at (16+12+6, y+10+0) = (34, y+10); amazon 24×24 at (34, y+16).\n\nWait — but text overrides INSIDE instance children: t1.characters=\"...\" — that's an override of a nested TEXT child — allowed ✓ (standard, works via findOne on instance).\n\nAlternatively — cleaner architecture: instead of instance+overrides, the Action/Account/Transaction components could expose the media via \"component properties\"? Overkill. Overlay approach is pragmatic and visually identical. The components still demonstrate reuse (4 tiles from 1 component, etc.).\n\nHmm wait, actually, let me reconsider: maybe swapComponent alone (without resize/x) preserves the layout overrides from the component (22×22 at 46,12) since those were set on the component's child instance — when you swap, Figma preserves instance overrides where properties match between old/new component? The docs for swapComponent: \"Swaps this instance for an instance of the given component. Attempts to preserve overrides.\" I believe size/position overrides ARE preserved (they're stored as overrides on the parent instance's children map...). Actually the icon inside tile2: tile2 is an instance; the icon child's size/pos in tile2 come from the component definition. Swapping the icon within tile2 — the swap applies