cceeded → frames created. Next call failed at clone (get null). And now frames don't exist. That means the build call's changes were rolled back retroactively?? \n\nOR: the clone call actually DID find f1 null because... the build call's changes were never committed because the MCP server commits asynchronously and the failure aborted the commit pipeline? That is: use_figma scripts run on a live session; changes commit on script completion. Build call completed successfully → should commit.\n\nHmm, alternatively the IDs: maybe when the 02/04 call ran `get(\"13:2\")` → null (some sync issue), the error rolled back... but why would f1 disappear from the CURRENT inspection?\n\nWhatever the mechanism — the practical lesson: a failed call can destroy work from the immediately-preceding call, OR frames were never truly persisted. Let me check: do a fresh inspection via get_metadata to confirm independent of the plugin session. Actually get_metadata uses the same file... let me just re-run the build for 01+03, then IMMEDIATELY in the SAME call build 02 and 04 too (single mega-call), so no cross-call dependency and no window for rollback weirdness. But a mega-call is riskier (one error rolls back everything).\n\nBetter strategy: rebuild 01+03 in one call, and if it succeeds, immediately do a SEPARATE tiny verification call (read-only) to confirm persistence BEFORE building 02/04. Then build 02/04 referencing IDs freshly discovered.\n\nBut the failure mode \"clone of null\" — why did get(\"13:2\") fail? Maybe because getNodeById doesn't work for nodes created in a different script session?? But 8:47 worked across calls (Card / Balance component created in Call A, used in build call). So cross-call getNodeById works generally. Unless... the 13:x IDs returned by the build call were the IDs of nodes in a transaction that got rolled back AFTER returning? \n\nHmm, one more theory: the build call succeeded and committed. Then the 02/04 call: get(\"13:2\") → null?? That call failed