thing rolls back and I retry with fixes. Mega-call risk is acceptable now. Keep reactions for a separate final call (they need frame IDs; but I can also do reactions within the same call since I have in-memory refs!). Yes — do EVERYTHING in one call: frames + reactions. Then verify.\n\nFor reactions inside the same call: need destination IDs — in-memory: f2.id, f1.id, f3.id, f4.id. \n\nReactions (new format):\n- btn1 (on f1): destination f2.id, navigation \"NAVIGATE\", transition MOVE_IN direction \"BOTTOM\"? Let me think about valid direction values: likely \"TOP\"|\"BOTTOM\"|\"LEFT\"|\"RIGHT\". For bottom sheet: sheet slides up from bottom → new screen enters from bottom → direction \"BOTTOM\" with MOVE_IN? Hmm, in Figma UI, \"Move In\" direction \"From bottom\"... Actually in the plugin API transition: {type:\"MOVE_IN\", direction:\"BOTTOM\"} means the destination moves in from the bottom edge. I'll try direction:\"BOTTOM\". If validation error, the whole mega-call rolls back — costly! \n\nRisk management: put reactions in a SEPARATE call after frames verified to persist. But cross-call persistence just proved flaky... Actually wait — was it? The components persisted across MANY calls reliably. The frames vanished. Maybe frames vanished because... hmm, actually here's a NEW theory: the failed clone call rolled back to the state at ITS start — and at its start, f1/f3 DIDN'T exist because the build call's changes were rolled back when the build call's session ended?? But then the return value showed IDs... IDs can exist transiently.\n\nUgh. Whatever — empirical approach: \n1. Mega-call: build all 4 frames + wire reactions, return all IDs.\n2. Immediately verify persistence with a read-only listing call.\n3. If persistent → screenshots.\n\nIf mega-call fails on reaction validation, I lose the frames build too — then split: frames call (no reactions), verify, reactions call. \n\nAlternative: do frames-only mega-call first, verify persistence, then reactions call. The