lso — hmm, why did get(\"13:2\") fail? One more possibility: IDs starting with \"13:\" — maybe there was an ID COLLISION or the returned IDs were from a different branch? Eh. Also possible: the MCP server's session state got reset between calls (the x-anthropic-billing header mentions sessions...). The components 8:x existed because they were committed to the FILE earlier; new 13:x nodes were in the session only. The \"verify persistence\" step will tell us.\n\nActually — realization: maybe use_figma commits changes only on script success AND the commit is what persists. Build call succeeded → committed. Clone call: get(\"13:2\") null. That's inconsistent with commit... UNLESS the commit takes a moment and the next call raced it. The clone call ran immediately after; race plausible! Then the listing call (3rd) ALSO showed no frames — race window over by then, should show frames... unless the commit FAILED.\n\nWhatever. Plan stands: mega-call, verify, react. Actually let me ALSO put the reactions in the mega-call but each in try/catch — saves a call, and catches format errors without rollback. If a catch swallows an error, I get the report in the return value and can fix in a follow-up call (which needs frame IDs — returned by mega-call; if cross-call get fails again, I'll use findOne by name as fallback for lookups!). Good idea: in follow-up calls, look up nodes via ws.findOne(n=>n.name===\"01_Onboarding\") instead of get(id) — more robust.\n\nWrite the mega-call now. Careful: in 02/04 sections use f1.clone() / f3.clone() in-memory. clone() of a frame with instances — fine.\n\nAlso note: f2 clone includes btn1 with its reaction (btn1 on 01 has reaction to f2 — the CLONE's btn would also carry reaction to f2... harmless; but the clone's button should go to f3! In clone, find the button instance inside f2 and re-point its reaction to f3. The clone's button: find via f2.findOne(n=>n.type===\"INSTANCE\"&&n.name===\"Button\")? The 1:23 component name — component \"1:23\"